# Vulnerabilities govulncheck reports that this repository has consciously
# accepted, one OSV id per line. Consumed by scripts/govulncheck.sh.
#
# An entry here suppresses a REAL finding. Nothing in this file means "false
# positive" — if a finding is wrong, the fix is a report to the Go vulnerability
# database, not a line here. Every entry states what the exposure is, what
# bounds it, and what has to happen for the line to be deleted.
#
# Format: the id, then any number of indented justification lines.

GO-2026-6452
  Panic via negative shared-string index in github.com/xuri/excelize.
  Reproduced against v2.11.0, the latest release, by
  internal/isbj/parse_malformed_test.go — this is a true positive.

  The GitHub advisory (GHSA-fx5j-qcqg-grpf) records
  "first_patched_version: 2.11.0" and is wrong: PR qax-os/excelize#2331 added
  the negative bounds check to xlsxC.getValueFrom but not to
  File.getFromStringItem (rows.go:359), which still tests only the upper
  bound. The Go vulnerability database is the accurate one — it lists no fixed
  version. Upstream master as of 2026-09-23 does not fix it either.

  Exposure: an authenticated user holding GovernmentFundingBills:Create in an
  organization uploads a workbook whose sharedStrings.xml exceeds
  UnzipXMLSizeLimit (25 MB) and whose cell carries t="s" with a negative <v>.

  Bounded by: internal/isbj now recovers the panic at the package boundary and
  returns an ordinary error, which the service layer already maps to a 400.
  gin.CustomRecovery (cmd/api/main.go) is the second net. No unauthenticated
  path reaches the parser, and nothing is disclosed — the failure mode was a
  panic, not a memory read.

  Why suppressed rather than fixed: govulncheck is a static call-graph
  analysis. It flags the call to GetRows, and recovering the panic does not
  remove that call. There is no version to upgrade to and no way to parse
  xlsx without GetRows, so the finding cannot be cleared by any change here.

  Delete this entry when excelize releases a version that bounds-checks
  getFromStringItem. scripts/govulncheck.sh warns once the finding stops
  appearing, which is the signal that the release has landed.
